{"annotations": [], "blocks": [{"bbox": [49.307, 204.95800000000008, 138.056, 217.47250000000008], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "heading", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 0, "source": "docling", "text": "1 Introduction"}, {"bbox": [49.30499999999998, 233.37700000000007, 290.25300000000004, 431.39490123456835], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 1, "source": "docling", "text": "Stable angina (SA), a clinical syndrome caused by myocardial ischemia, remains a significant global health concern. It is associated with a 3-4% annual risk of myocardial infarction or death and aects an estimated 9.6% of the adult population in China, imposing a substantial burden on both healthcare systems and patient quality of life (1, 2). Current guidelinedirected medical therapy-including lifestyle modifications, pharmacotherapy, and revascularization-aim to alleviate symptoms, improve exercise tolerance, and reduce the risk of cardiovascular events (3). However, the eÿcacy of these treatments is often incomplete, with a considerable proportion of patients continuing to experience anginal symptoms and activity limitations despite optimal medical management (4). Moreover, the long-term use of conventional antianginal drugs can lead to tolerability issues and adverse eects (AEs), highlighting the need for eective complementary therapeutic strategies (5)."}, {"bbox": [219.6679999999999, 74.20999999999992, 545.369, 108.45500000000004], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 2, "source": "docling", "text": "Conclusion: Adjunctive treatment with XZOL for 12 weeks significantly reduced angina pain intensity and the need for rescue medication in patients with stable angina, with a favorable safety profile."}, {"bbox": [219.66799999999992, 118.03999999999996, 516.3889999999999, 126.38300000000004], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 3, "source": "docling", "text": "Clinical trial registration: https://clinicaltrials.gov/, ChiCTR1900026899."}, {"bbox": [219.668, 144.68200000000002, 250.26999999999998, 149.731], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 4, "source": "docling", "text": "KEYWORDS"}, {"bbox": [219.668, 154.832, 545.3720000000001, 172.13599999999997], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 5, "source": "docling", "text": "Chinese herbal formula, Chinese medicine, randomized controlled trial, stable angina, Xuefu Zhuyu oral liquid"}, {"bbox": [304.4069999999999, 197.87, 545.3730000000003, 372.09190123456824], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 6, "source": "docling", "text": "stasis' (QBS)-a CM diagnostic pattern that conceptually aligns with the blood flow obstruction and ischemic pain observed in SA (8). Xuefu Zhuyu oral liquid (XZOL) is a modern, standardized formulation of this decoction, manufactured under Good Manufacturing Practice (GMP) standards and approved by the China Food and Drug Administration (Approval No. Z10950063). It is composed of 11 herbs, including Bupleuri Radix (Chaihu), Persicae Semen (Taoren), Paeoniae Radix Rubra (Chishao), Aurantii Fructus Immaturus (Zhiqiao), Rehmanniae Radix (Shengdihuang), Achyranthis Bidentatae Radix (Niuxi), Angelicae Sinensis Radix (Danggui), Carthami Flos (Honghua), Glycyrrhizae Radix et Rhizoma (Gancao), Platycodonis Radix (Jiegeng) and Chuanxiong Rhizoma (Chuanxiong) (Supplementary Table 1 in Supplementary File 1)."}, {"bbox": [49.30499999999989, 435.6430000000005, 290.253, 586.0689012345687], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 7, "source": "docling", "text": "Chinese Medicine (CM) has emerged as a promising adjunctive approach for managing SA. A systematic review suggested that combining conventional treatment with CM for angina pectoris may oer benefits in symptom and serum cholesterol reduction, but the evidence is limited (6). More recently, a largescale real-world study demonstrated that an integrated medicine approach significantly reduced cardiovascular events compared to conventional treatment alone; however, the study's single-center design and the heterogeneity of the CM interventions limit the generalizability of these fi ndings (7). Collectively, these studies indicate the therapeutic potential of CM but also underscore the ongoing controversy surrounding its clinical application, which stems from a lack of high-quality, definitive evidence."}, {"bbox": [49.30500000000001, 590.3170000000007, 290.25300000000004, 621.7629012345686], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 8, "source": "docling", "text": "Among the numerous herbal formulas, Xuefu Zhuyu decoction, a classical prescription, is traditionally used for conditions characterized by 'Qi stagnation and Blood The therapeutic rationale for XZOL in SA is supported by a growing body of preclinical evidence. A pharmacological study has demonstrated that key components, including Chuangxiong Rhizoma, Carthami Flos, Rehmanniae Radix, Aurantii Fructus, and Glycyrrhizae Radix et Rhizoma , substantially inhibit adenosine diphosphate (ADP)-induced platelet aggregation (9). Animal models have further revealed their multi-target eects, including anti-atherosclerotic actions, reduction of serum lipids and inflammation, repair of endothelial injury, and stabilization of atherosclerotic plaques-all of which are critical mechanisms in the pathophysiology of coronary artery disease (10-12). Although a meta-analysis of earlier, smaller trials suggested that the Xuefu Zhuyu formula is eective for angina pectoris (13), this evidence is undermined by significant methodological limitations. Therefore, a rigorously designed, large-scale, placebo-controlled trial is essential to definitively establish the eÿcacy and safety of XZOL. This study was designed to address that evidence gap."}, {"bbox": [49.301999999999964, 647.487, 290.2490000000002, 779.5139999999999], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 9, "source": "docling", "text": "Abbreviations: SA, stable angina; AE, adverse event; CM, Chinese medicine; QBS, Qi stagnation and Blood stasis; XZOL, Xuefu Zhuyu oral liquid; GMP, Good Manufacturing Practice; ADP, adenosine diphosphate; CONSORT, Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials; CCS, Canadian Cardiovascular Society; PCI, percutaneous coronary intervention; VAS, visual analog scale; IBRCM, Institute of Basic Research in Clinical Medicine; IWRS, Interactive Web Response System; ACE, angiotensin-converting enzyme; ARB, inhibitors, angiotensin II receptor blocker; CCB, calcium channel blocker; DSMC; Data and Safety Monitoring Committee; SAE, serious adverse event; EDC, electronic data capture; SAQ, Seattle Angina Questionnaire; PSQI, Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index; EQ-5D-5L; EuroQol5-Dimensions-5Leve; WHO-UMC, World Health Organization-Uppsala Monitoring Centre; ITT, intention-to-treat; FAS, full analysis set; SS, safety set; PPS, per-protocol set; PRO, patient-reported outcome; ECG, electrocardiogram; HDL-C, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol."}, {"bbox": [304.425, 610.49, 370.359, 623.0045], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "heading", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 10, "source": "docling", "text": "2 Methods"}, {"bbox": [304.425, 637.9739999999999, 394.28099999999995, 649.098], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "heading", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 11, "source": "docling", "text": "2.1 Study design"}, {"bbox": [304.423, 665.31, 545.3710000000003, 780.0419012345678], "bold": null, "confidence": 1.0, "font_size": null, "kind": "paragraph", "ocr_engine": null, "page": 2, "seq": 12, "source": "docling", "text": "This multicenter, double-blind, randomized, placebocontrolled trial was conducted between June 2020 and June 2022 at six sites ascross China. The study protocol was approved by the Ethics Committees at Guangdong Provincial Hospital of Chinese Medicine (Approval No. BF2019-175-01) and was registered in the Chinese Clinical Trial Registry (ChiCTR1900026899). The trial is reported in accordance with the Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials (CONSORT) Extension for Chinese Herbal Medicine Formulas 2017 reporting guideline (14). The study protocol has been previously published (15)."}], "checksum": "fc291e59987aeeff0e868a8d4486dcc29df50fe49e3f0363079e296d7f0da21e", "content_present": true, "doc_id": "d-e546a005ed1bb895", "docling_version": "2.118.0", "engine_version": "2.118.0", "errors": [], "images": [], "page_index": 1, "page_sizes": {"2": [595.2760009765625, 841.8900146484375]}, "route": "docling", "schema_version": "v0.1.0", "source_hash": "e546a005ed1bb8957707c4a76f66c3aea22f3a70c49f1649ed76f2f4c6a4e94e", "status": "ok", "tables": [], "timings": {}}